==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|douȝter in lawe}}; equivalent to {{af|en|daughter|-in-law}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈdɔːtə(ɹ)ɪnˌlɔː/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-PerfectSoundWhatever-daughter-in-law.wav|a=CA}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|head=[[daughter]]-[[in-law]]|daughters-in-law|daughter-in-laws|pl2qual=colloquial, nonstandard}}

# {{senseid|en|Q18489480}}The [[wife]] of one's [[child]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Lauren M. Johnson and Christina Zdanowicz|title=A gravestone missing for almost 150 years was being used as a marble slab to make fudge|magazine=w:CNN|url=https://www.cnn.com/2021/09/24/us/gravestone-returned-after-missing-150-years-trnd/index.html|date=2021-09-24|passage=They discovered Weller had two daughters and a '''daughter-in-law''' in the same cemetery.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2024-11-17|author=Howie Klein|title=A Plurality Of American Voters Have Embraced Kakistocracy As Their Preferred Form Of Governance|work=DownWithTyranny|url=https://www.downwithtyranny.com/post/a-plurality-of-american-voters-have-embraced-kakistocracy-as-their-preferred-form-of-governance
|passage=And we can watch how that plays out as Trump attempts to get his '''daughter-in-law''', Lara, into the U.S. Senate.}}

